1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,839
Ryde. Multi-Agent Autonomous Dispute Resolution System. This is FairTrip, a working prototype built with CodeBuddy for ride-hailing disputes.

2
00:00:07,839 --> 00:00:14,372
FairTrip represents both the rider and the driver, gathers evidence, and explains the recommended outcome.

3
00:00:14,372 --> 00:00:21,340
This edited walkthrough covers the project, two end-to-end dispute categories, human oversight, and lessons from development.

4
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:27,438
The route-deviation and no-show demonstrations were captured from live model runs during this walkthrough.

5
00:00:27,438 --> 00:00:30,922
Later examples use clearly labelled recorded case reviews.

6
00:00:30,922 --> 00:00:38,761
All cases are synthetic, the rules are a demonstration policy, and the prototype does not execute real payments.

7
00:00:40,261 --> 00:00:43,944
The intended users are riders, drivers, and support operations.

8
00:00:43,944 --> 00:00:51,308
A rider may challenge an unexpected detour, while a driver may maintain that a no-show charge was justified.

9
00:00:51,308 --> 00:00:56,628
Instead of asking one assistant to accept either account, FairTrip assigns separate advocates.

10
00:00:56,628 --> 00:01:04,401
Both advocates request GPS, chat, payment, history, and policy records through tools, then build cited arguments and acknowledge counterevidence.

11
00:01:04,401 --> 00:01:12,175
The Judge reviews both cases. The Risk Analyst summarises signals calculated by the server; it does not decide fault.

12
00:01:12,175 --> 00:01:19,949
The React interface shows the hearing, while an Express and TypeScript backend orchestrates the agents and validates their outputs.

13
00:01:19,949 --> 00:01:24,041
The model selects a remedy. The server calculates the money.

14
00:01:25,541 --> 00:01:28,479
Here is the first dispute category: route deviation.

15
00:01:28,479 --> 00:01:34,724
In the unapproved-detour scenario, the rider says the driver took a much longer route and charged more.

16
00:01:34,724 --> 00:01:38,397
The driver says the alternative was intended to avoid congestion.

17
00:01:38,397 --> 00:01:41,335
We select the scenario and start the review.

18
00:01:41,335 --> 00:01:47,947
The hearing lines show the advocates gathering evidence independently, preparing their cases, and handing them to the Judge.

19
00:01:47,947 --> 00:01:53,456
The Judge considers the actual route, the reference route, the conversation, and the demonstration policy.

20
00:01:53,456 --> 00:01:57,129
In this live capture, the rider's claim is upheld and

21
00:01:57,129 --> 00:02:00,803
the recommended refund is one Singapore dollar and ninety-eight cents.

22
00:02:00,803 --> 00:02:03,741
The independent policy check agrees with the Judge.

23
00:02:03,741 --> 00:02:07,414
The amount comes from server-side calculations using the recorded fare.

24
00:02:08,914 --> 00:02:13,954
The evidence is available for inspection rather than hidden inside a generated answer.

25
00:02:13,954 --> 00:02:17,831
Selecting a citation takes the reviewer to the underlying record.

26
00:02:17,831 --> 00:02:21,708
Route evidence compares the actual journey with a reference route.

27
00:02:21,708 --> 00:02:26,360
Conversation records show whether the rider approved or objected to the route.

28
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:29,849
Payment records show the fare and eligible refund calculation.

29
00:02:29,849 --> 00:02:35,277
Each advocate can cite only records it retrieved, and the backend validates those references.

30
00:02:35,277 --> 00:02:39,154
Prompts require structured JSON and explicit evidence and policy identifiers.

31
00:02:39,154 --> 00:02:44,582
The Judge must choose from permitted remedy identifiers and cannot supply a monetary amount.

32
00:02:44,582 --> 00:02:49,234
This separates generated reasoning from the calculations that determine the recommended action.

33
00:02:50,734 --> 00:02:53,300
The second category is a no-show charge.

34
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:57,331
We select the case where pickup and waiting conditions were met.

35
00:02:57,331 --> 00:03:00,996
The rider says they were only a few minutes late.

36
00:03:00,996 --> 00:03:05,394
The driver says they arrived, contacted the rider, and waited before cancelling.

37
00:03:05,394 --> 00:03:12,358
Again, both advocates retrieve the evidence. This time, the pickup record, waiting interval, and contact attempts support the driver.

38
00:03:12,358 --> 00:03:17,122
In the live capture, the Judge upholds the charge and recommends no refund.

39
00:03:17,122 --> 00:03:23,353
The policy check agrees. FairTrip therefore demonstrates outcomes in favour of either party, depending on the evidence.

40
00:03:23,353 --> 00:03:28,118
It does not assume that filing a complaint means a refund is deserved.

41
00:03:28,118 --> 00:03:33,982
Both parties receive explanations, and reviewers can inspect the arguments and policy checks behind the result.

42
00:03:35,482 --> 00:03:40,701
This next example is a recorded case review, labelled as such in the interface.

43
00:03:40,701 --> 00:03:46,667
The trip conditions support keeping the no-show charge, but the driver's history produces a high-risk assessment.

44
00:03:46,667 --> 00:03:51,887
The case is sent for human review, and the displayed amount remains a proposal.

45
00:03:51,887 --> 00:03:56,361
Risk never establishes fault and does not automatically reverse the trip-based recommendation.

46
00:03:56,361 --> 00:04:01,953
The connected backend supports reviewer confirmation and remedy overrides, with amounts rebuilt by the server.

47
00:04:01,953 --> 00:04:07,173
Completed and reviewed cases can become precedents for later cases, as consistency context only.

48
00:04:07,173 --> 00:04:12,393
A live attempt at this history variation was interrupted during this walkthrough; we are

49
00:04:12,393 --> 00:04:17,613
showing the saved successful review here, not presenting it as a fresh live result.

50
00:04:19,113 --> 00:04:21,959
FairTrip also shows when it cannot decide.

51
00:04:21,959 --> 00:04:28,057
In this recorded missing-route-evidence case, the GPS data needed to assess the detour is unavailable.

52
00:04:28,057 --> 00:04:32,530
The result is incomplete, with no ruling or payment amount issued.

53
00:04:32,530 --> 00:04:38,222
Separately, the interrupted live history-variation attempt showed a workflow failure after the automatic re-run.

54
00:04:38,222 --> 00:04:43,507
The interface reported that the decision was unavailable and issued no payment amount.

55
00:04:43,507 --> 00:04:51,232
These are distinct states: missing evidence requires more facts, while a failed workflow requires another attempt or operational intervention.

56
00:04:51,232 --> 00:04:54,891
Policy disagreement and uncertain outcomes also require human review.

57
00:04:54,891 --> 00:04:58,957
Instruction-like text inside chat evidence is treated as case material,

58
00:04:58,957 --> 00:05:03,023
and history and precedents cannot override the current trip evidence.

59
00:05:04,523 --> 00:05:12,380
The submission folder includes a saved live evaluation from the thirteenth of October, twenty twenty-six, using MiniMax M3.

60
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:16,308
All twenty-seven evaluation runs passed across twenty synthetic fixtures.

61
00:05:16,308 --> 00:05:23,292
The scorecard recorded passing monetary correctness, citation validity, trace completeness, and risk-signal checks across the runs.

62
00:05:23,292 --> 00:05:28,967
Three repeated fixtures passed the consistency check, and the human-review feedback probe passed.

63
00:05:28,967 --> 00:05:36,824
Median latency was sixteen point one seconds, and the maximum was nineteen point two seconds in that evaluation.

64
00:05:36,824 --> 00:05:43,808
These are results from that recorded evaluation, not a claim that every future request will succeed.

65
00:05:43,808 --> 00:05:47,736
Today's interrupted example illustrates why operational failure handling matters.

66
00:05:47,736 --> 00:05:52,101
Production savings and customer-satisfaction improvements have not yet been measured.

67
00:05:53,601 --> 00:05:58,022
The repository includes genuine CodeBuddy development-chat screenshots covering setup, GPS

68
00:05:58,022 --> 00:06:02,444
calculations, API integration, a running interface, live evaluation, and debugging.

69
00:06:02,444 --> 00:06:09,076
The build approach used small task cards, a written specification, and checks after each step.

70
00:06:09,076 --> 00:06:14,381
Shared contracts defined the interface between agent orchestration and the evidence layer.

71
00:06:14,381 --> 00:06:17,476
Golden-value tests checked calculations against specified outcomes.

72
00:06:17,476 --> 00:06:22,782
A practical prompting lesson was to show the exact object shape required

73
00:06:22,782 --> 00:06:28,088
for counterevidence: argument objects with claims and citations, rather than plain strings.

74
00:06:28,088 --> 00:06:33,393
A practical development tip is to give the coding agent a bounded

75
00:06:33,393 --> 00:06:38,699
task and explicit verification commands, then inspect the output before moving on.

76
00:06:38,699 --> 00:06:43,120
Keep arithmetic in tested functions, validate generated structures at boundaries,

77
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:47,541
and preserve traces so that a failure can be diagnosed.

78
00:06:49,041 --> 00:06:58,034
FairTrip is currently a local prototype with synthetic cases, a demonstration policy, in-memory records, and no authenticated reviewer identity.

79
00:06:58,034 --> 00:07:05,134
Live model calls depend on provider availability, and confidence is model-assessed rather than calibrated accuracy.

80
00:07:05,134 --> 00:07:14,127
The next steps are persistent storage, authenticated support workflows, real platform evidence and policy integrations, and broader operational evaluation.

81
00:07:14,127 --> 00:07:18,860
The intended business value is less manual evidence review, more

82
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:23,594
consistent policy application, and clearer explanations for riders and drivers.

83
00:07:23,594 --> 00:07:30,693
Those benefits still need production measurement. FairTrip demonstrates two complete dispute workflows, inspectable evidence, constrained

84
00:07:30,693 --> 00:07:37,793
monetary calculations, and a clear path to human oversight when the system cannot safely finish.
